WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.557
Real batches contain sequences of different lengths, but tensors need a consistent shape.

00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:11.932
Padding adds special tokens to shorter examples so they match the longest example in the batch.

00:00:11.932 --> 00:00:18.252
Truncation removes tokens when an input exceeds the permitted maximum length or the limit you set.

00:00:18.252 --> 00:00:24.147
In the Python example, two sentences are tokenized together with padding, truncation,

00:00:24.147 --> 00:00:26.877
and return_tensors set to PyTorch.

00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:29.472
Print the keys, the input shape, and the attention mask.

00:00:29.472 --> 00:00:36.752
You should see a rectangular tensor even though the original sentences have different lengths.

00:00:36.752 --> 00:00:41.427
The attention mask distinguishes real tokens from padded positions.

00:00:41.427 --> 00:00:45.122
Truncation deserves special attention in real projects.

00:00:45.122 --> 00:00:50.167
If a document contains an important sentence near the end and you truncate it away,

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:53.507
the model cannot use information it never receives.

00:00:53.507 --> 00:00:59.062
Long-document handling therefore becomes an architectural and data-design decision,

00:00:59.062 --> 00:01:00.989
not just a tokenizer option.
